    <string name="shizuku_note_reboot">Shizuku sessions end at every reboot. If actions stop working, start Shizuku again.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_steps_title">Set up Shizuku (wireless debugging)</string>
    <string name="shizuku_step_1">1. Enable Developer options: Settings → About phone → tap Build number 7 times.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_step_2">2. In Developer options, enable Wireless debugging and keep the phone on the same Wi-Fi as your computer.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_step_3">3. Open Shizuku and choose the wireless debugging pairing option. Android shows a pairing code and port.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_step_4">4. Shizuku asks you to pair over ADB. Accept the prompts, then return to BatchKit.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_step_5">5. Tap Check again here. The status chip turns green when the connection works.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_troubleshooting_title">OEM quirks</string>
    <string name="shizuku_trouble_miui">Xiaomi / HyperOS: enable Developer options → USB debugging (Security settings) and turn off MIUI optimization if Shizuku keeps dying.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_trouble_realme">Realme / OPPO / ColorOS: turn off Developer options → Disable permission monitoring, otherwise Shizuku loses permission after a while.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_trouble_generic">Other OEMs: some ROMs kill background services aggressively. Disable battery restrictions for Shizuku itself.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_trouble_usb">Prefer USB: enable USB debugging, connect to a computer with ADB, then run the command Shizuku shows in its app.</string>
    <string name="shizuku_help_note">BatchKit talks to Shizuku only. It has no internet permission and never sends data anywhere.</string>

